 instance just because it got unlucky so  to meet these needs we built our own  auto scalar and I highly recommend this  if you're not if the if the stock  autoscaler is not working for you it's  actually pretty easy to build with just  a few GC p components so we have here a  cloud scheduler which kicks off a cloud  function every minute or so and that  cloud  it's just a few hundred lines of  JavaScript so the cloud function is  doing a couple of things it reaches out  to stackdriver and at us first of all  for the demand so how many jobs do we  have running in progress and how many  jobs do we currently have queued and  then it looks at the supply side of  things how many workers do we currently  have connected or that are currently  scaling out if it decides that we need  to scale up additional capacity it can  do this really simply by just reaching  out to the instance group manager API  there and it can just say please hit the  target size for this instance group 2x  if it wants to scale down it puts a  message on the pub sub Q and then  whenever a worker is idle it simply  checks this queue to see if there's a  shutdown message for that particular  worker class and the unlucky worker that  drew the short straw just e registers  itself from the instance group manager  and then it terminates gracefully so  this architecture allows us to ensure  there were any other terminating workers  that are idle another thing that we  needed to consider when running this  many instances is the networking so we  ran all of our instances and a private  end of PPC unless users private IP  addresses and when you're using private  ip's it means that if you need to reach  the outside Internet then you need to go  through an app google has a product  called cloud net which went GA back in  November but when we set this up cloud  net was not available so we actually had  to manually create a whole bunch of Nats  and that meant we needed to create a net  and every availability zone basically in  every region and some availability zones  needed more than one net to handle the  bandwidth throughput that we needed and  this works but it's do infrastructure  that we don't want to manage so we're  currently in the process of migrating to  cloud net and cloud net can  automatically scale horizontally to  support your demand also if you're doing  any communication with private with with  managed Google services you have to  enable private Google access so private  Google access allows your machines that  are running on private IP addresses to  route directly to those services like  bigquery or GCS or spanner allows you to  route directly to those services without  needing to go through your net so it  significantly reduces the bottleneck on  your net private Google access is  actually pretty complicated to set up if  you're using if you're managing your  instances but if you're using cloud net  it's it's a checkbox when you set up  your V PC so check that box and you will  live a happy life the final picture of  the the final piece of the picture here  is storage so as I mentioned we're using  Google Cloud Storage for storing inputs  and output data and we're using Google  container registry to store our runtime  container images and Google Cloud  Storage we have things like application  binaries map tiles machine learning  models and sensor data all of these  things that we need to run a simulation  we've gotten pretty amazing throughput  from GCS so this is our right throughput  in a typical day we might be peeking it  sort of a gigabyte per second but we  don't write nearly as much data as we  read so this is our read throughput and  you can see on a typical day  we're peeking it over 380 gigabytes per  second of data so that's over 3 terabyte  and this is an example of where the  cloud is super powerful because if we  won't if we set out to build  infrastructure like this ourselves it  would be incredibly challenging to  manage reliably at that scale and it  would also not be value-add work for a  cruise instead we simply click a button  in the console or write a few lines of  terraform and we have this kind of  capacity available to us instantly we  have a bunch of work coming down the  pipeline for Hydra so we're always  making performance improvements to  handle the increasing load we're looking  at tasks pipelining so that we can  prefetch assets for the next job that  we're about to run before we've finished  the current job well we're optimizing  where we where we send jobs based on  which workers already have resources  cached and we're a be testing our worker  updates so that we can make sure that  make sure that workers are working  successfully and then we haven't  introduced any regressions and our work  infrastructure before we start managing  production load so the last thing I'm  going to talk about is our data  analytics pipeline if you recall from  this previous slide I mentioned that  we're storing an output data in and  Google Cloud storage and then we ingest  this into bigquery and like with Hydra I  think it's helpful to touch briefly on  the architecture of where we came from  before I talk about how we're using  bigquery so in this diagram you can see  our simulation job scheduler that's  Hydra and Hydra is pulling work from the  facts and from other simulation data  that we need and then the output data  that it produces this goes into our  graph compute engine and the graph  computer engine allows engineers to run  post-processing tasks like they might  want to for example compare that output  data against human labelled ground truth  data and create aggregations on that to  score how well we did in that simulation  the post-processing data was then stored  as ever if I was an s3 and we chose ever  here for a couple reasons first of all  it was just really easy to work with  Hydra already had the ability to save  output files and a s3 and secondly we  didn't need to worry about schemas so  our engineers could make changes to the  AV code or they can make changes to  their post-processing steps and that  data would get written directly to every  files which they were then able to  access without needing to go through any  team or get approval to change their  schemas we had to lake we have tools  like jupiter notebooks and also custom  analysis tooling which can could  directly read these ever files and then  render them to create sorts of  dashboards but this architecture became  pretty limiting once we started wanting  to run more advanced analysis without a  proper query engine here we couldn't  answer advanced queries without going  back and editing those post-processing  code and this also created pretty  significant memory use front-end  visualization tools needed to read these  ever files and it did not scale well as  the number of simulations increased  finally it's pretty time consuming  building this type of tolling if you're  tying it specifically to the data format  that you're using there's many different  ways we could have gone about solving  this but given that we were using Google  Cloud we decided to use bigquery as the  data warehouse so we have an ETL  pipeline here that's a simple ingestion  service and that's taking the data  produced by our post-processing graph  compute engine and ingest this into  bigquery we've had great success with  bigquery so far so we're ingesting over  10 terabytes a day of data with no  administration overhead to us we can get  this data into bigquery within minutes  of finishing a simulation and we can  query it within seconds and the bigquery  sequel interface is accessible and  supported by many different reporting  tools not just our in-house ones now  engineers can access this data via the  bigquery console and UI we also have  things like Jupiter node  and bi toes like looker in tableau they  can access it and we also have our own  custom front-end analysis tooling so the  last thing I'm going to talk about today  is a bit about this this this custom  analysis tooling that we build  I'll give you an insight into the world  of self-driving car development so I'm  gonna walk through a complete example of  a simulation and how we run that and how  we understand the results so here bear  with me here we have a car trying to  make an unprotected left turn and the  car needs to yield to these cars in the  oncoming lane of traffic so this is  represented in pink we also have what we  call a region of interest which is the  dotted line that you see and alkyne  needs to clear this region of interest  between that gap and traffic between  those two cars what we're interested in  this particular example is which gap our  card chose to go through and what the  the gap size was what were the period of  time was and the gap size is the period  of time between when car 1 the blue car  has left that region of interest and  when car two the green car entered that  region of interest okay so if you're  still with me keep that in mind we move  on to the next slide this is an example  of some of the custom visualization  tooling that we built so you're in what  we're looking at the gap size between  the two cars as we're making an  unprotected left turn and in this  particular example we're comparing two  factors we're looking at how the speed  of those oncoming cars and the distance  between those oncoming cars affects the  gap that we chose and the size of that  gap and you can see here that we're  comparing a base branch and a feature  branch and then in the third box you can  see the Delta between the base and  feature branch so engineers can easily  identify with this particular commit how  that impacted which gap we chose as a  function of these two input parameters  each one of these green squares here is  actually a separate individual  simulation so that's running on hydro  and that's generating probably a  gigabyte of output data each so you can  see how we end up with these large-scale  computer and data storage challenges  once we start running these types of  simulations another thing about this  tool is that the engineers can actually  click on one of these particular squares  and it gives them more information about  that particular run so we have a lot of  ongoing work in this in the simulation  tooling like this  we're closely with our AV engineering  teams to understand what type of  experiments they want to run and how we  can best sort of understand and  visualize those experiments so thank you  for joining today before I go I want to  leave you with three thoughts so the  first one simulation for self-driving  cars is a super interesting problem it's  a privilege to get to work on it at  cruise we're using the latest tools and  infrastructure to build to test these  cars at massive scale so this includes  things like Google cloud and also  in-house develop software and finally it  takes a diverse range of skill sets to  build self-driving cars so you know we  have not just machine learning engineers  and robotics engineers but back in  engineers infrastructure engineers 3d  front-end engineers data data science  and we're hiring in San Francisco in  Seattle if you're interested in working  on these types of problems every day so  [Music]
 

